Make the vif-hotplug udev rule work on a Shepherd (systemd-less) system.

udev-qubes-network.rules (installed as 99-qubes-network.rules) reconfigures a
VM's interface when dom0 dynamically (de)attaches a vif at runtime.  Upstream
asks systemd to (re)start qubes-network-uplink@<iface>.service.  On a native
Guix template there is no systemd/systemctl/templated unit.

Bridge the hotplug event into Shepherd instead: the ADD action runs
`herd reconfigure qubes-network-uplink <iface>`, a re-runnable custom action on
the existing uplink service.  Routing through Shepherd reuses its serialized,
ordered action dispatch (the direct analog of upstream's udev->systemd), so the
rule needs no in-script locking; the action is idempotent and no-ops if the
interface has already vanished.  REMOVE does nothing: the vanished netdev drops
its own kernel addresses/routes, and the next add reconfigures.

Guix-specific: systemd distributions need the systemctl form.

Upstream file: QubesOS/qubes-core-agent-linux, network/udev-qubes-network.rules.
diff --git a/network/udev-qubes-network.rules b/network/udev-qubes-network.rules
index 208bf15..e6d146b 100644
--- a/network/udev-qubes-network.rules
+++ b/network/udev-qubes-network.rules
@@ -1,8 +1,16 @@
 # new udev has DRIVERS
-SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="add", DRIVERS=="vif", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl restart --job-mode=replace qubes-network-uplink@%k.service", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
-SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="remove", DRIVERS=="vif", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl stop --job-mode=replace qubes-network-uplink@%k.service", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
+# On a Shepherd (systemd-less) system there is no systemctl and no
+# qubes-network-uplink@<iface> templated unit.  Bridge the hotplug event
+# into Shepherd: `herd reconfigure qubes-network-uplink <iface>` runs the
+# uplink (re)configuration through Shepherd's serialized, ordered action
+# dispatch (the analog of upstream udev->systemd), so no in-script locking
+# is needed.  The action is idempotent and no-ops if the interface has
+# already vanished.  remove: nothing (the vanished device drops its own
+# kernel addrs/routes; the next add reconfigures).
+SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="add", DRIVERS=="vif", RUN+="/run/current-system/profile/bin/herd reconfigure qubes-network-uplink %k", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
+SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="remove", DRIVERS=="vif", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
 # old udev has ENV{NET_ID_DRIVER}
-SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="add", ENV{NET_ID_DRIVER}=="vif", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl restart --job-mode=replace qubes-network-uplink@%k.service", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
-SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="remove", ENV{NET_ID_DRIVER}=="vif", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl stop --job-mode=replace qubes-network-uplink@%k.service", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
+SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="add", ENV{NET_ID_DRIVER}=="vif", RUN+="/run/current-system/profile/bin/herd reconfigure qubes-network-uplink %k", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
+SUBSYSTEMS=="xen", KERNEL=="eth*", ACTION=="remove", ENV{NET_ID_DRIVER}=="vif", GOTO="QUBES-NET-END"
 
 LABEL="QUBES-NET-END"
